  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.greedy_color - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.greedy_color.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.greedy_color()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Color the graph using a greedy algorithm.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.greedy_color()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Color the graph using a greedy algorithm.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.greedy_color()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Color the graph using a greedy algorithm.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nColor the graph using a greedy algorithm.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L75636-L75857){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.greedy_color(\n    strategy=\"largest_first\",\n    interchange=False,\n    seed=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Greedy coloring](../assets/graphs/greedy-color.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A strip of four triangles on six nodes, each node filled with the color of its class: C and F in the accent color, A and D in teal, B and E in violet. No edge joins two nodes of the same color, and the card lists the three classes 0, 1, 2.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nGraph coloring assigns colors (represented as integers) to nodes such that no two adjacent\nnodes share the same color. The greedy algorithm processes nodes in a specified order and\nassigns the smallest available color to each node. The result is always a valid coloring,\nbut not necessarily one with the fewest possible colors; the node ordering chosen by\n`strategy=` largely determines how many colors end up being used.\n\nColoring is a way of splitting nodes into conflict-free groups. Typical uses include:\n\n- scheduling (exams, meetings, or jobs that conflict get different time slots)\n- register allocation and frequency assignment (interfering items get different slots)\n- partitioning work into batches where no two items in a batch interact\n- getting a quick upper bound on the chromatic number (see\n  `chromatic_number_upper_bound()`)\n\nEdge direction is ignored: in a directed graph, `A -> B` still forbids `A` and `B` from\nsharing a color. Self-loops are ignored. Every node in the graph receives a color, and\nisolated nodes always get color `0`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[strategy]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;largest_first&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   The strategy for ordering nodes during coloring. Options are: `\"largest_first\"` (the\n    default), `\"smallest_last\"`, `\"saturation_largest_first\"` (alias `\"dsatur\"`),\n    `\"independent_set\"`, `\"random\"` (alias `\"random_sequential\"`, as in NetworkX), and\n    `\"arbitrary\"`.\n    Matching is case-insensitive. See the *Strategy Types* section for how each one\n    works. An unrecognized name raises a `ValueError`.\n\n<code>[interchange]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to apply the color-interchange optimization. When a node would otherwise need\n    a brand-new color, the algorithm first tries to free an existing color by swapping\n    two colors along a *Kempe chain* (a connected run of nodes using just those two\n    colors), which keeps the coloring valid. This usually saves colors at a modest extra\n    cost, though as a heuristic it can occasionally use one more. It works with the\n    `\"largest_first\"`, `\"smallest_last\"`, `\"random\"`, and `\"arbitrary\"` orderings; DSATUR\n    and `\"independent_set\"` choose their own order and raise a `ValueError` when combined\n    with it. Default is `False`.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Random seed for the `\"random\"` strategy. The same seed always gives the same coloring\n    of the same graph; if `None` (the default), the order is drawn from system entropy,\n    so repeated calls can differ. Ignored by the other strategies.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[int, int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A dictionary mapping every node ID to its assigned color (0-indexed integers).\n    The colors are consecutive integers starting from 0.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `strategy=` is not one of the recognized names, or if `interchange=True` is\n    combined with `\"saturation_largest_first\"`/`\"dsatur\"` or `\"independent_set\"`.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_valid_coloring`) \n\n:   Check that no edge joins two nodes of the same color.\n\n[](`~chromatic_number_upper_bound`) \n\n:   The number of colors a coloring uses.\n\n[](`~clique_number`) \n\n:   A lower bound on the number of colors needed.\n\n[](`~edge_coloring`) \n\n:   Color the edges instead of the nodes.\n\n## Strategy Types {.doc-strategy-types}\n\nThere are several strategies that can be used:\n\n- `\"largest_first\"`: process nodes in order of decreasing degree; handles the most\nconstrained nodes early and is fast\n- `\"smallest_last\"`: repeatedly remove a minimum-degree node, then color in the reverse\nof that removal order; often produces optimal or near-optimal colorings on sparse graphs\n- `\"saturation_largest_first\"` or `\"dsatur\"`: DSATUR algorithm that always colors next the\nnode with the most differently-colored neighbors (ties broken by degree); often produces\nthe fewest colors\n- `\"independent_set\"`: repeatedly extract a maximal independent set from the uncolored\nnodes (favoring high-degree nodes) and give the whole set one color\n- `\"random\"` or `\"random_sequential\"`: shuffled node ordering; pass `seed=` for a\nreproducible result, or leave it unset to get a different ordering on each call\n- `\"arbitrary\"`: process nodes in the graph's internal storage order\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nGreedy coloring is what every graph-coloring course starts with and what most real\nsoftware uses in practice, despite its worst-case wastefulness. For any ordering it\nuses at most $\\Delta(G) + 1$ colors, where $\\Delta$ is the maximum degree, since a\nnode can never have more than $\\Delta$ already-colored neighbors. Even a bipartite\ngraph (which needs only $2$ colors) can receive many more under an unlucky ordering.\nThe `strategy=` argument controls this crucial ordering step. *DSATUR* (Brélaz,\n1979) is the strongest general-purpose choice: it colors the highest-saturation node\nnext, meaning the node whose neighborhood already uses the most distinct colors, and\nconsistently produces optimal or near-optimal colorings for typical graphs.\n*smallest_last* peels the minimum-degree node last and applies its coloring in\nreverse peeling order, matching DSATUR on many sparse inputs; *largest_first* is\nfaster but coarser. Because the underlying problem is $\\mathsf{NP}$-hard, none of\nthese strategies guarantees the true chromatic number, but running several and\ntaking the best is a cheap and effective heuristic.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nAround a square, two colors are enough: A and C get color 0, and B and D get color 1, so no\nedge joins two nodes of the same color. The result maps each node ID to its color, and\nstoring it as an attribute puts the colors next to the labels:\n\n::: {#5480bd26 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"A\")]\n)\n\ncoloring = graph.greedy_color()\ngraph.set_node_attrs(\"color\", coloring)\ngraph.get_node_df().select(\"label\", \"color\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (4, 2)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>label</th><th>color</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;A&quot;</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;B&quot;</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C&quot;</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;D&quot;</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Scheduling exams\n\nIn the exams network, two courses are joined when some student takes both, so their exams\ncan't share a time slot. A coloring is a timetable: each color is a slot, and no student has\ntwo exams at once. Storing DSATUR's coloring as an attribute and drawing it:\n\n::: {#2d976de2 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams = ec.exams()\n\nslots = exams.greedy_color(strategy=\"dsatur\")\nexams.set_node_attrs(\"slot\", slots).plot(\n    color_by=\"slot\", label_by=\"label\", seed=3, height=420\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"420\" viewBox=\"30.0 12.4 691.9 415.1\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neu